---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { path } from '../lib/paths';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';
---

<Page
  title="Privacy"
  description="What MathemAct stores, who processes it, and how to have it erased."
  lead="What is stored, who else sees it, and how to get rid of it. In plain terms, because a privacy notice nobody reads protects nobody."
  updated="August 2026"
>
  <h2>Who is responsible</h2>

  <p>
    MathemAct is a personal project. The data controller is an individual
    based in Italy, not an institution. Requests about personal data, rights under the GDPR,
    and anything else covered here go to{' '}
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What is stored</h2>

  <dl class="items">
    <dt>Your email address</dt>
    <dd>
      Needed to confirm the account, to sign you in, and to reset a password. It is also
      what your institutional badge is derived from. It is <strong>never displayed</strong>
      on the site, never returned by the API, never included in the public export, and
      never shown to moderators.
      <p class="legal-basis">Legal basis: performance of the account contract (GDPR Article 6(1)(b)).</p>
    </dd>

    <dt>Your display name</dt>
    <dd>
      Shown on everything you post. It may be a pseudonym, at every verification tier.
      <p class="legal-basis">Legal basis: performance of the account contract (Article 6(1)(b)).</p>
    </dd>

    <dt>Your institution, if the domain matched</dt>
    <dd>
      Derived automatically from your confirmed email domain against the Research
      Organization Registry, along with the date of that check. Stored as the institution
      name and country. You cannot set or edit this yourself.
      <p class="legal-basis">Legal basis: performance of the account contract (Article 6(1)(b)).</p>
    </dd>

    <dt>What you post</dt>
    <dd>
      Reports, entries, comments, ratings, and confirmations that a report still
      reproduces, with the date
      each was made. Ratings are stored per person so that you can change yours; individual
      ratings are never shown attributed to a name.
      <p class="legal-basis">Legal basis: performance of the account contract (Article 6(1)(b)).</p>
    </dd>

    <dt>Your activity record</dt>
    <dd>
      A list of what you did here — posted, rated, flagged, commented — and of what
      happened to it afterwards: a moderator's decision, somebody's comment, somebody's
      rating. You can read it under <a href={path('/account/activity/')}>your activity</a>,
      and nobody else can, including moderators. It records the event and the date, never
      an email address and never anything a moderator wrote to another moderator. It is
      deleted outright when your account is erased, along with the note of when you last
      looked at it.
      <p class="legal-basis">Legal basis: performance of the account contract (Article 6(1)(b)).</p>
    </dd>

    <dt>Moderation decisions about you</dt>
    <dd>
      When a moderator decides whether something stays up, the reason they wrote is stored
      and shown to two people: whoever posted the content, and whoever flagged it. You can
      read the ones addressed to you under
      <a href={path('/account/#decisions')}>moderation decisions</a>. They never name the
      moderator, and — where the content is yours — they never say who flagged it. They are
      deleted outright when your account is erased; the record that a decision was taken
      stays in the moderators' own log, without your content attached to it.
      <p class="legal-basis">Legal basis: legitimate interests in running a moderated site (Article 6(1)(f)), and performance of the account contract (Article 6(1)(b)).</p>
    </dd>

    <dt>An erasure request, if you make one</dt>
    <dd>
      The fact that your account has asked to be erased, when it was asked, and anything
      you chose to add. Only you and an administrator can see it, and withdrawing the
      request deletes the record of it outright.
      <p class="legal-basis">Legal basis: compliance with the right to erasure under GDPR Article 17.</p>
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <h3>What is not stored</h3>

  <p>
    There is no analytics of any kind, no advertising, no tracking pixels, and no
    fingerprinting. Nothing about your visit is recorded while you read: the pages are
    static files, and the database is contacted only when you sign in or post something.
    Fonts are served from this site rather than from anyone else's font service.
  </p>

  <p>
    Exactly one script is loaded from another company, on exactly four pages: Cloudflare's
    Turnstile check, where you sign up, sign in, reset a password, or ask for another
    confirmation link. It is not on any page you only read, and you can confirm that by
    viewing source.
  </p>
  <p class="legal-basis">Legal basis: prevention of automated abuse — legitimate interests (GDPR Article 6(1)(f)).</p>

  <p>
    There is no cookie banner because there are no persistent cookies. Being signed in is
    kept in this browser's own local storage, written when you sign in and removed when you
    sign out. Two small numbers sit beside it for the same reason — whether this account
    moderates, and how many notifications were waiting last time a page asked — so that the
    header can be drawn without every page you read contacting the database. Both are
    cleared when you sign out, and neither leaves the browser.
  </p>

  <h2>Retention</h2>

  <p>
    Account data — your email address, display name, and institutional record — is held for
    as long as your account exists. On erasure it is deleted within a reasonable time,
    typically the same day. Content you posted is detached from your account on erasure (see
    below) and is retained as part of the corpus, which is its purpose.
  </p>

  <p>
    Brevo retains logs of the emails it sent on your behalf for as long as the account is
    active. Cloudflare's Turnstile privacy addendum does not state a specific retention
    period for the signals it processes during bot checks. GitHub retains server logs
    according to its own privacy statement.
  </p>

  <h2>Who processes it</h2>

  <p>Four services are involved. Each receives only what it needs to do its job.</p>

  <dl class="items">
    <dt>Supabase</dt>
    <dd>
      Database and authentication, hosted in the European Union on AWS infrastructure.
      Holds everything listed above. This is the only processor that sees the whole picture.
      Supabase operates under a DPA incorporating Standard Contractual Clauses for any
      transfers outside the EU. Supabase's sub-processor list is published at{' '}
      <a href="https://supabase.com/legal/customer-resources/subprocessor-list">supabase.com/legal</a>.
    </dd>

    <dt>Brevo</dt>
    <dd>
      Sends the confirmation and password-reset emails, and therefore receives your email
      address and the contents of those messages. Nothing else. It is never used to send
      announcements. Brevo is a French company and stores data within the EU.
    </dd>

    <dt>Cloudflare</dt>
    <dd>
      Provides the Turnstile check that keeps automated signups out. It receives your IP
      address, TLS fingerprint, and browser user agent when you sign up, sign in, ask for
      a password reset, or ask for another confirmation link. Its script is loaded on those
      four pages and nowhere else, so it is never involved in a page you only read. The
      Turnstile privacy addendum requires that it be referenced here:{' '}
      <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/turnstile-privacy-policy/">cloudflare.com/turnstile-privacy-policy</a>.
    </dd>

    <dt>GitHub</dt>
    <dd>
      Serves the site's static files. GitHub records your IP address as part of any
      ordinary web request. No account is required to read this site. See GitHub's{' '}
      <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">privacy statement</a>.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <h2>What is public</h2>

  <p>
    Everything you post is public, and is published under a{' '}
    <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" rel="license">CC BY 4.0</a>{' '}
    licence. It is also included in a{' '}
    <a href={`${SITE.repository}/tree/main/data`}>nightly export</a> that anyone can download
    in full, as JSON and as CSV. Assume that anything you post can be copied, archived,
    quoted, and analysed by people you did not anticipate, because it can. Your email address
    is not in the export — there is no field in it that could hold one.
  </p>

  <p>
    Take particular care with transcript excerpts. Remove anyone else's unpublished
    material before you paste, and remember that a transcript may contain more identifying
    detail than you noticed when you were in the conversation.
  </p>

  <h2>Erasing your account</h2>

  <p>
    Ask on the <a href={path('/account/delete/')}>erasure page</a> while signed in, and it
    will be done. Your profile, email address, and institutional record are deleted
    outright. Asking from within the account means nobody has to prove who they are by
    email, and it means the request cannot be made by someone else on your behalf. If you
    cannot sign in, write to <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a
    > from the address the account uses.
  </p>

  <p>
    Content you posted is detached from you rather than deleted with you: the body of a
    report or comment is removed and the author attribution is stripped, but the thread
    structure stays, so that the discussion around it does not collapse into
    incomprehensibility. If you want specific posts deleted as well, say so and they will
    be. Anything already downloaded by someone else from a previous export cannot be
    recalled, and neither can a copy of that export already in the site's public history,
    which is true of anything published anywhere.
  </p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>

  <p>
    Under the GDPR you have the right to: access your personal data; have it corrected; have
    it erased; restrict how it is processed; receive a portable copy; and object to
    processing based on legitimate interests. Write to{' '}
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a> and a response will follow
    within one month. If a request is refused you will be told why.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you believe your data has been handled unlawfully, you have the right to complain to
    the supervisory authority for Italy:{' '}
    <strong>Garante per la protezione dei dati personali</strong>,{' '}
    Piazza Venezia 11, 00187 Roma, Italy.{' '}
    <a href="https://www.garanteprivacy.it">garanteprivacy.it</a>
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>

  <p>
    If this notice changes in a way that affects what is collected or who sees it,
    registered users will be told before it takes effect, not after. The date at the top
    is the date of the last change, and the full history is in the{' '}
    <a href={SITE.repository}>public repository</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Questions about any of this, including the awkward ones, go to{' '}
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a>. The{' '}
    <a href={path('/about/')}>about page</a> explains how verification works in more detail.
  </p>
</Page>

<style>
  .items {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .items dt {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-md);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
  }

  .items dd {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-3xs);
    margin-inline-start: 0;
  }

  .legal-basis {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-3xs);
    margin-block-end: 0;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    font-size: var(--size-2);
    font-style: italic;
  }
</style>
